### ImageMagick version

4b0ce67c9a20fe6d72ed59b7dc327b1042f57c4f

### Operating system

Linux

### Operating system, version and so on

20.04

### Description

### Description
The `getenv` function is used to read environment variables `SRCDIR` and `MAGICK_FONT`, which are external inputs controllable by the user. The `SRCDIR` variable is used to construct a file path that is passed directly to the `Image` constructor to read a file. Due to the lack of validation and sanitization for these inputs, an attacker could manipulate the environment variables to include path traversal sequences (e.g., `../../`) to access arbitrary files. This leads to potential unauthorized access to sensitive files through the application's output.

### Affected Code Snippet
```cpp
// Method: main#7#65#shapes.cpp
int main( int , char ** argv) {
    try {
        string srcdir("");
        if(getenv("SRCDIR") != 0)
            srcdir = getenv("SRCDIR");
        Image image( "300x300", "white" );
        std::vector<Coordinate> poly_coord;
        poly_coord.push_back( Coordinate(30,30) );
        poly_coord.push_back( Coordinate(100,10) );
        poly_coord.push_back( Coordinate(190,290) );
        poly_coord.push_back( Coordinate(30,290) );
        Image texture( srcdir + "tile.miff" );
        image.fillPattern( texture );
        // Further processing...
    }
    catch( exception &error_ ) {
        cout << "Caught exception: " << error_.what() << endl;
        return 1;
    }
}
```

### Vulnerability Type
- CWE-552: Files or Directories Accessible to External Parties

### Version
This vulnerability has been identified in version `4b0ce67c9a20fe6d72ed59b7dc327b1042f57c4f`.

### Steps to Reproduce

export SRCDIR="../../etc/"
./shapes

### Images

dont need

--- COMMENTS ---
[c1] @urban-warrior 2026-01-22T03:07:37Z:
ImageMagick uses this pattern in its demos and test utilities, but these executables are **not** included in a standard installation. When the code is used independently, developers can harden it by sanitizing any user‑controlled paths before passing them to ImageMagick.

As an additional layer of protection, a user may restrict ImageMagick’s filesystem access by denying all reads except those from explicitly allowed directories. A [security policy](https://imagemagick.org/script/security-policy.php) such as the following can enforce this:

```xml
<policymap>
  <!-- Deny all file reads by default -->
  <policy domain="path" rights="none" pattern="*" />

  <!-- Allow reads ONLY from /opt/ImageMagick/ -->
  <policy domain="path" rights="read" pattern="/opt/ImageMagick/*" />
</policymap>
```

How this policy works

- Global deny rule — Blocks all filesystem reads (pattern="*").
- Allow rule — Re‑enables read access only for files located under /opt/ImageMagick/.

All other paths, including system directories such as /etc/, /home/, /var/, and any relative path traversal attempts like ../../,  are denied.
---

